 ## Overview
-WebGL Gaussian Splatting is a real-time rendering technique for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) scenes in web browsers, typically implemented using WebGL 1.0 and libraries like Three.js [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of displaying large-scale, photorealistic radiance field reconstructions on standard client hardware without requiring neural network inference or heavy server-side processing [S1, unofficial]. Unlike parent method [Gaussian Splat Rendering (Screen-Space Quad)](2075-gaussian-splat-rendering-screen-space-quad.md), which relies on a pre-sorted order buffer and view-space projection data, this method performs depth sorting in a JavaScript Web Worker and projects 3D Gaussians into screen-space quads using custom vertex and fragment shaders [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling [Gaussian Splat Fragment Shader](3258-gaussian-splat-fragment-shader.md) by providing the complete pipeline, including data parsing, instance matrix construction, and CPU-side sorting, rather than just the shading logic [S2, unofficial].
+WebGL Gaussian Splatting is a real-time rendering technique for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) implemented in web browsers using WebGL 1.0 and the Three.js library [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of displaying large-scale, photorealistic 3D scenes reconstructed via Gaussian Splatting on client-side hardware without requiring neural network inference or server-side ray tracing [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings by utilizing a CPU-side depth sorting algorithm (often offloaded to a Web Worker) to resolve the non-commutative nature of alpha blending, whereas the parent [Gaussian Splat Rendering (Screen-Space Quad)](2075-gaussian-splat-rendering-screen-space-quad.md) describes the general projection and quad-drawing mechanism without specifying the sorting implementation [S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Animated 3D Gaussian Splatting (Splatv rendering)](7758-animated-3d-gaussian-splatting-splatv-rendering.md), this method typically omits spherical harmonics to reduce file size and memory footprint for web delivery [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A binary `.splat` file containing Gaussian parameters (position, scale, quaternion, color, opacity) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Output:** A rendered image of the Gaussian splats composited via alpha blending [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** A binary `.splat` file containing $N$ Gaussian primitives. Each primitive consists of a 3D center, 3D scale, quaternion rotation, and color/alpha data [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** A 2D screen image rendered via WebGL 1.0 `drawElements` or `drawArrays` with instanced rendering, using custom vertex and fragment shaders [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
