+### [Isotropic Gaussian Point-Sprite Rendering](6949-isotropic-gaussian-point-sprite-rendering.md)
+A simpler variant using isotropic point sprites with CPU-side back-to-front sorting to correct blending order, lacking anisotropic covariance and spherical harmonics.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting is $O(N \log N)$ using JavaScript's native `.sort()` method, performed in a Web Worker to avoid blocking the main thread [S2, unofficial]. Rendering is $O(N \cdot P)$ where $P$ is the number of pixels covered by quads.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing splat data and instance matrices [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Real-time on ordinary graphics hardware, significantly faster than NeRFs for inference [S1, unofficial]. Performance depends on vertex count and viewport resolution.
-*   **Strengths:** No external dependencies beyond WebGL 1.0 and Three.js; works in standard web browsers; supports progressive loading [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Does not support view-dependent shading (spherical harmonics) in this basic implementation to reduce file size [S1, unofficial]. Precision issues may arise with very large scenes or extreme aspect ratios if not handled by the culling bounds [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Web-based visualization of 3DGS scenes where server-side rendering is not feasible.
-*   **When not to use:** When view-dependent effects are required or when higher precision is needed than WebGL 1.0 provides [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting is $O(N)$ with counting sort, but dominated by the constant factor of memory access and worker communication. Rendering is $O(N)$ for draw calls.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing splat data and sorted indices.
+*   **Performance:** Real-time on modern desktop GPUs via WebGL 1.0. Performance scales with the number of splats $N$ and viewport resolution.
+*   **Strengths:** No external dependencies beyond Three.js; works on standard web browsers; efficient on ordinary graphics hardware compared to NeRFs [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** No view-dependent shading (spherical harmonics omitted for file size); limited to WebGL 1.0 features; sorting on CPU can block main thread if not offloaded [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Web-based visualization of static or slowly moving 3DGS scenes.
+*   **When not to use:** High-fidelity view-dependent effects; scenes with extreme depth complexity requiring tile-based rasterization; mobile devices with poor WebGL 1.0 support.
 
 ## Background
-The technique was popularized by the "3D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" paper, which introduced the method for generating photorealistic 3D scenes from images [S1, unofficial]. The specific WebGL implementation by antimatter15 demonstrated that 3DGS could be rendered efficiently on ordinary graphics hardware by extending point cloud rendering techniques, avoiding the neural network inference required by NeRFs [S1, unofficial]. It was implemented in JavaScript with WebGL 1.0 to ensure broad compatibility [S1, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 as a real-time radiance field rendering technique [S1, unofficial]. WebGL implementations, such as the one by antimatter15, adapted this technique for the web using WebGL 1.0 and Three.js, focusing on efficient point-cloud-like rendering without neural network inference [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to allow interactive viewing of 3DGS models in browsers without heavy dependencies [S1, unofficial].
 
